package builtin

// The functions Tack provides without a declaration. They are available in
// every file without an import.
//
// The compiler holds no list of them: it reads this directory, every .tck
// file in it, from $TACK_HOME/src/std/builtin, and each extern func
// declared here is a builtin. Every declaration in this directory must be
// one: an extern func with its C name given, starting tack_, that returns
// nothing, and whose parameters are of basic types; anything else is an
// error naming the file. A call to a builtin is an ordinary call to its C
// function.
//
// The body of each is C, in a .c file beside this one, named after the
// builtin and defining its C function: print.c defines tack_builtin_print.
// The driver compiles each .c file here as it compiles a program's own,
// and links only those that define a builtin the program calls, and the
// ones those need in turn, so a program that calls neither print nor exit
// links no C library code for them. Anything not yet implemented belongs
// in PLANNED.md instead.

// print writes s to standard output exactly as given, adding no newline.
extern("tack_builtin_print") func print(s string)

// exit ends the process immediately with the given status. Pending defers
// do not run, in the calling function or any other. The status is an
// int32, which C's exit takes as its int; an int64 status needs a
// conversion first. Only the low bits of the status reach the operating
// system, the low 8 on Linux and macOS, so exit(70000) ends with status
// 112.
extern("tack_builtin_exit") func exit(code int32)

// len(v) returns the number of elements of the array, view or list v, or
// the number of bytes of the string v, as an int64. Its parameter takes
// any array, view, list or string, which no Tack declaration can say, so
// the compiler implements it itself and it is described here rather than
// declared. The length of an array, and of a string literal, is a
// constant; a view's or a list's is never known.

// make(list[T], alloc Allocator) list[T] returns an empty list of
// elements of type T, which grows through the allocator alloc and is
// freed through it when its owner's block ends. It allocates nothing, so
// it cannot fail. It takes a type, which no Tack declaration can, so the
// compiler implements it itself.

// append(mut l list[T], v T) error appends v to the end of l, growing its
// buffer through l's allocator when it is full, and returns no error; or,
// when the allocator cannot grow it, returns error{code: 1, msg: "out of
// memory"} and leaves l as it was. Its error cannot be dropped. It takes
// a list of any element type, which no Tack declaration can say, so the
// compiler implements it itself.

// c_allocator, a keyword, is the Allocator that lowers to C's malloc,
// realloc and free, the one Allocator there is. Allocator, a builtin
// type, is an opaque handle on an allocator, which a list holds; it is
// copyable, and has no zero value and no ==.
